# HFT Elements - Implementation Guidelines

## Project Context
This is a high-frequency trading (HFT) system for converting Market-By-Order (MBO) feeds to Market-By-Level (MBL) feeds. Performance is critical - every nanosecond matters. The system must process 1M+ events/sec with P95 latency < 5µs.

## Core Principles

### 1. Type Safety - NO PRIMITIVE TYPES
**ALWAYS use type aliases from `hft_elements/common/types.h`**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
void process_order(uint32_t price, uint32_t qty, uint64_t order_id);
float calculate_midpoint(float bid, float ask);
int64_t timestamp = get_time();
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
void process_order(Price price, Quantity qty, OrderId order_id);
Price calculate_midpoint(Price bid, Price ask);
Timestamp timestamp = RdtscTimer::now();
```

**Rationale:** Strong types prevent bugs (e.g., passing quantity where price is expected). The compiler catches type mismatches at compile time.

### 2. Cache-Line Alignment - Prevent False Sharing
**ALWAYS align hot-path structures to 64 bytes**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
struct PriceLevel {
    uint32_t price;
    uint32_t quantity;
};

class OrderBook {
    std::atomic<uint64_t> seq;  // May share cache line with other data
    uint64_t event_count;
};
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
struct alignas(64) PriceLevel {
    Price price;
    Quantity quantity;
    bool is_dirty;
    uint8_t padding[55];  // Pad to 64 bytes
};

class OrderBook {
    alignas(64) std::atomic<uint64_t> seq;  // Own cache line
    alignas(64) uint64_t event_count;       // Own cache line
};

static_assert(sizeof(PriceLevel) == 64, "Must be cache-line sized");
```

**Rationale:** False sharing causes cache-line ping-pong between cores, destroying performance. Each frequently-written atomic should have its own cache line.

### 3. Lock-Free > Wait-Free > Mutex
**Prefer lock-free data structures, NEVER use mutex in hot path**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
std::mutex mutex_;
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    update_book(event);
}

std::shared_mutex rw_lock_;
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
// Use SeqLock for single-writer, multiple-reader
atomic_primitive::SeqLock lock_;
{
    atomic_primitive::SeqWriteGuard guard(lock_);
    update_book(event);
}

// Or use atomic operations with proper memory ordering
std::atomic<uint64_t> counter_;
counter_.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
```

**Rationale:** Mutexes involve kernel syscalls and context switches (100s of ns). Lock-free structures use atomic CPU instructions (single-digit ns).

### 4. NO Virtual Functions - Zero Vtable Overhead
**Use templates for polymorphism, NEVER virtual in hot path**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
class IBookManager {
    virtual void process_event(const MboEvent& event) = 0;
    virtual ~IBookManager() = default;
};

class BookManager : public IBookManager {
    virtual void process_event(const MboEvent& event) override;
};
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
// Use templates for compile-time polymorphism
template<typename BookManagerT>
class SnapshotGenerator {
    BookManagerT& book_manager_;
public:
    void generate() {
        book_manager_.process_event(event);  // Direct call, no vtable
    }
};

// Or CRTP if inheritance needed
template<typename Derived>
class BookManagerBase {
    void process_event(const MboEvent& event) {
        static_cast<Derived*>(this)->process_event_impl(event);
    }
};
```

**Rationale:** Virtual calls require vtable lookup (indirect jump, cache miss, branch misprediction). Templates resolve at compile time.

### 5. Memory Allocation - Pre-allocate, No Dynamic Allocation in Hot Path
**Allocate once at initialization, reuse in hot path**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
void process_event() {
    std::vector<PriceLevel> levels;  // Allocates on heap
    levels.push_back(level);
    
    std::string json = build_json();  // Dynamic allocation
}
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
class Processor {
    // Pre-allocated at construction
    std::array<PriceLevel, MAX_LEVELS> levels_;
    std::array<char, MAX_JSON_SIZE> json_buffer_;
    
    void process_event() {
        // Use pre-allocated buffers, no allocation
        size_t count = fill_levels(levels_);
        build_json_into(json_buffer_);
    }
};
```

**Rationale:** malloc/free are slow (syscalls, lock contention). Pre-allocation eliminates this overhead.

### 6. NUMA Awareness - Pin Threads, Allocate Local Memory
**Always pin worker threads to CPU cores**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
std::thread worker([&]() {
    // Thread can migrate across NUMA nodes
    process_events();
});
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
std::thread worker([&]() {
    // Pin to specific CPU core
    CpuUtils::pin_to_core(core_id);
    
    // Allocate on local NUMA node
    int numa_node = CpuUtils::get_numa_node(core_id);
    void* buffer = CpuUtils::numa_alloc(size, numa_node);
    
    process_events();
});
```

**Rationale:** Cross-NUMA memory access is 2-3x slower than local. Thread migration causes cache invalidation.

### 7. Memory Ordering - Use Weakest Sufficient Ordering
**Don't use seq_cst by default, choose appropriate ordering**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
// seq_cst is expensive (full memory barrier)
counter_.fetch_add(1);  // Defaults to seq_cst
flag_.store(true);      // Defaults to seq_cst
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
// Use relaxed for counters (no ordering needed)
counter_.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);

// Use acquire/release for synchronization
flag_.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
if (flag_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) { ... }
```

**Rationale:** seq_cst emits expensive memory fences. Relaxed/acquire/release are faster and sufficient for most cases.

### 8. Inlining - Help the Compiler
**Mark hot-path functions inline or use templates**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
// In .cpp file, won't be inlined
Price get_best_bid() {
    return bid_levels_[0].price;
}
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
// In header, compiler can inline
inline Price get_best_bid() const noexcept {
    return bid_levels_[0].price;
}

// Or force inline for critical functions
[[gnu::always_inline]] inline Price get_best_bid() const noexcept {
    return bid_levels_[0].price;
}
```

**Rationale:** Function call overhead (push/pop stack frame) is significant at nanosecond scale. Inlining eliminates this.

### 9. Branch Prediction - Help the CPU
**Use likely/unlikely hints for predictable branches**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
if (error_condition) {  // Rarely true, but CPU doesn't know
    handle_error();
}

if (likely_condition) {  // Usually true
    fast_path();
} else {
    slow_path();
}
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
if (__builtin_expect(error_condition, 0)) {  // Tell CPU: unlikely
    handle_error();
}

if (__builtin_expect(likely_condition, 1)) {  // Tell CPU: likely
    fast_path();
} else {
    slow_path();
}

// Or use macros
#define LIKELY(x)   __builtin_expect(!!(x), 1)
#define UNLIKELY(x) __builtin_expect(!!(x), 0)

if (UNLIKELY(error_condition)) { ... }
```

**Rationale:** Branch mispredictions cause pipeline stalls (10-20 cycles). Hints improve prediction accuracy.

### 10. Data Structure Layout - Cache-Friendly Access Patterns
**Arrange data for sequential access, not random**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
// Array of pointers (cache-unfriendly)
std::vector<Order*> orders;
for (auto* order : orders) {
    process(order);  // Each access is a cache miss
}

// Data scattered in map
std::map<OrderId, Order> orders;  // Tree nodes scattered in memory
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
// Array of values (cache-friendly)
std::array<Order, MAX_ORDERS> orders;
for (const auto& order : orders) {
    process(order);  // Sequential access, prefetcher works
}

// Flat hash map (better cache locality)
std::unordered_map<OrderId, Order> orders;
orders.reserve(expected_size);  // Pre-allocate
```

**Rationale:** Modern CPUs prefetch sequential data. Random access (pointers, trees) defeats prefetching.

### 11. Avoid Unnecessary Copies - Use References and std::move
**Pass by const reference, move when possible**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
void process_event(MboEvent event) {  // Copies 64 bytes
    // ...
}

std::string json = builder.build();
send(json);  // Copies string
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
void process_event(const MboEvent& event) noexcept {  // No copy
    // ...
}

std::string json = builder.build();
send(std::move(json));  // Move, no copy
```

**Rationale:** Copying data is expensive. References and moves eliminate copies.

### 12. Compiler Optimization Hints
**Use noexcept, const, constexpr where possible**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
Price calculate_midpoint(Price bid, Price ask) {
    return (bid + ask) / 2;
}
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
constexpr Price calculate_midpoint(Price bid, Price ask) noexcept {
    return (bid + ask) / 2;
}
```

**Rationale:** noexcept allows better optimization (no exception handling code). constexpr enables compile-time evaluation. const allows caching.

### 13. Static Linking Only - No Dynamic Libraries
**All libraries must be static (.a), not shared (.so)**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cmake
add_library(hft_common SHARED ${SOURCES})
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cmake
add_library(hft_common STATIC ${SOURCES})

# Executables must statically link C++ runtime
set(CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS "${CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS} -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++")
```

**Rationale:** 
- Dynamic linking adds PLT/GOT indirection overhead (indirect jumps, cache misses)
- Static linking enables whole-program optimization and better inlining
- Static C++ runtime linking ensures binaries are portable across systems (GCC 14 binary runs on GCC 11 system)
- No dependency on host system's libstdc++ version
- Faster startup - no dynamic linker resolution
- Better cache locality - all code in one place

**What is statically linked:**
- ✅ All project libraries (libhft_*.a)
- ✅ libgcc (GCC runtime)
- ✅ libstdc++ (C++ standard library)

**What remains dynamically linked (intentionally):**
- glibc (system C library - must match host)
- libnuma (NUMA support)
- pthread (POSIX threads)

**Verification:**
```bash
# Binary should NOT list libstdc++.so.6
ldd ./build/release/examples/utilities/catdbn/catdbn
```

### 14. Measure, Don't Guess - Use Metrics
**Always add metrics for hot paths**

❌ **FORBIDDEN:**
```cpp
void process_event(const MboEvent& event) {
    update_book(event);
}
```

✅ **REQUIRED:**
```cpp
void process_event(const MboEvent& event) {
    auto start = RdtscTimer::now();
    
    update_book(event);
    
    auto elapsed = RdtscTimer::now() - start;
    latency_histogram_.record(RdtscTimer::cycles_to_ns(elapsed));
}
```

**Rationale:** You can't optimize what you don't measure. Metrics identify bottlenecks.

## Code Review Checklist

Before submitting code, verify:

- [ ] No primitive types (uint32_t, float, etc.) in function signatures
- [ ] All hot-path structures are 64-byte aligned
- [ ] No mutex/lock_guard in hot path
- [ ] No virtual functions in hot path
- [ ] No dynamic allocation (new/malloc) in hot path
- [ ] Worker threads are CPU-pinned
- [ ] Atomic operations use appropriate memory ordering (not seq_cst by default)
- [ ] Hot functions are inline or template
- [ ] Branch hints (likely/unlikely) on predictable branches
- [ ] Data structures use sequential access patterns
- [ ] Pass by const reference, use std::move
- [ ] Functions marked noexcept where appropriate
- [ ] Metrics added for critical paths
- [ ] Static assertions for size assumptions (static_assert)

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

1. **std::string in hot path** → Use fixed buffers or string_view
2. **std::cout/printf in hot path** → Use async logging
3. **std::chrono for timing** → Use RDTSC (nanosecond precision)
4. **std::vector.push_back in loop** → Pre-allocate with reserve()
5. **Passing structs by value** → Pass by const reference
6. **Default-constructed atomics** → Specify memory ordering explicitly
7. **Unaligned data access** → Use alignas() for proper alignment
8. **Mixing hot and cold data in same cache line** → Separate with alignas(64)

## When in Doubt

1. Check if similar code exists in the codebase - follow that pattern
2. Benchmark before and after changes - measure impact
3. Use perf tools to identify bottlenecks (perf stat, perf record)
4. Consult docs/PLAN.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md for design decisions

